/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*-------------------------------*\
  envelope04_water_benzene_two_liquids -- a P-T envelope whose bubble curve
  is a PHANTOM, and the probe that says so.

  WHY THIS CASE EXISTS (EX-02, 2026-11-15).  The Explorer offered the
  envelope for water + benzene on an ideal liquid and drew a clean
  single-liquid envelope: bubble 362.2 K at 1 bar, while the mixture splits
  into two liquids and starts to boil on the heteroazeotrope line, near
  342 K at 1 atm.  The lens now takes the T-x-y's activity models and, for a
  binary on a non-ideal model, runs the T-x-y's own liquid-stability probe
  on the envelope's pressure grid.  This is that run, on UNIFAC + PR.

  WHAT THE RUN SHOWS (native build, 2026-11-15):

    * the single-liquid envelope: bubble 327.755 K at 1 bar, 26 bubble
      points (the march ends at 27 bar, where the incipient vapour has no
      vapour root of the cubic: CL-01, 2026-11-16; before it kept 80 points
      to 80 bar, the later ones on the cubic's pseudo-root); the dew branch keeps 38 points and is ended at
      39 bar by a reported root jump;
    * the probe finds TWO LIQUIDS at the feed's bubble point at all 80
      pressures of the grid (liquid_stable = 0 everywhere): the homogeneous
      bubble curve above is a phantom over the whole range it keeps, and the lens draws none of it
      as a curve (each point a hollow x);
    * the dew curve is NOT probed (the probe tests the liquid at the bubble
      point only).

  NOT VALIDATED: no number here is checked against a measured envelope; the
  probe's verdict rests on UNIFAC's predicted split.  Structural witness of
  the lens's two-liquid flag; its counts are the golden rows.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

application   choupoProps;
description  "P-T envelope of equimolar water + benzene on UNIFAC + PR beside the T-x-y's liquid-stability probe: two liquids at the bubble point at all 80 pressures, so the single-liquid bubble curve is a phantom the lens does not draw";
verbosity     2;
